A||N||4||fierovette||Proper&best way to replace coolant ||||5||
Z||000000||fierovette||07-16-2001||08:34 AM||||I never replaced coolant in mt 88GT. Couldnt find anything in the past teck questions. What is the most efficent, proper,and complete way to do this? Also how much,and what type of antifreze is needed? Should the system be flushed? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||07-16-2001||12:28 PM||||Okay, no one else stepped up.  This is a plug (no pun intended) for the Ogre.  He has generously amassed an amazing amount of technical wizardry.  It will explain all if you follow the link at the top of the page to the Ogre's Cave.  Coolant stuff is under the engine category, I believe.<BR>Gary||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||07-16-2001||08:08 PM||||That cooling article is soon to be updated...<P>If you are flushing it forget the section about coolant premix. That part is for DRY motors, like when you just changed an engine. (Don't buy it premixed either)<P>After flushing drain the system. Radiator and coolant pipes under car. <P>Find out the total system capacity and devide it in half. Put in that amount of fresh UNDILUTED coolant. (This is 7 quarts of new coolant for nearly all models. [half of 13.8 according to haynes. 6.9 quart of new coolant to be exact for a V6.])<P>Once that coolant is in top off the system with water according to the filling instructions. <P>This will get you much closer to a 50/50 mix. Many people forget about trapped water after a flush. If you pour in premix you'll be too weak for the coolant to work right.<P>Don't forget to clean out and refill the recovery tank with 50/50 mix.<P>Just take the tank out and clean it. there is likely allot of crap in the bottom. Plus you can check for cracks etc...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierovette||07-17-2001||08:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>That cooling article is soon to be updated...<P>If you are flushing it forget the section about coolant premix. That part is for DRY motors, like when you just changed an engine. (Don't buy it premixed either)<P>After flushing drain the system. Radiator and coolant pipes under car. <P>Find out the total system capacity and devide it in half. Put in that amount of fresh UNDILUTED coolant. (This is 7 quarts of new coolant for nearly all models. [half of 13.8 according to haynes. 6.9 quart of new coolant to be exact for a V6.])<P>Once that coolant is in top off the system with water according to the filling instructions. <P>This will get you much closer to a 50/50 mix. Many people forget about trapped water after a flush. If you pour in premix you'll be too weak for the coolant to work right.<P>Don't forget to clean out and refill the recovery tank with 50/50 mix.<P>Just take the tank out and clean it. there is likely allot of crap in the bottom. Plus you can check for cracks etc...<P>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-16-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As usual, help is always out there, THANKS.<P>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Kwez||07-19-2001||09:13 PM||||hihihi, I just could not resist :<P>I found a nice & easy way to flush the coolant Wednesday (w. my 1.5 week old 88GT) ...<P>just wait for the water pump to die at 1:00am on a country road ;-)<BR>all you have to do is watch the green stuff flow while waiting for  the tow truck.<P>if there's no street lights, just listen to the sound & hope you went to wee wee before 'cause it's gonna make you wanna go ;-)<P>keep smiling||||reg||1
